8
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:35,760
And the last book is titled
The Problem of Being Too Good.

9
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:38,200
When I started in this world...

10
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:41,760
Not in the world of psychology,
but the other world.

11
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:42,920
I was an actor.

12
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:45,160
I dedicated myself to the world of show business.  I

13
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:46,680
was an artist

14
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:49,640
and, in addition, a
radio and television presenter...

15
00:00:49,720 --> 00:00:52,160
In other words,
my life was directed towards the

16
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:55,920
field of communication
and entertainment.

17
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,760
A bohemian life, they said at my house.

18
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:01,560
This happened in the years..., he

19
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:07,160
must have been 18,
until 28 or 30, and possibly,

20
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:10,320
when
the Barcelona Olympics took place.

21
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:14,080
And after the Olympics, in 1993, there was

22
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:19,360
an economic recession,
maybe the older ones can remember it,

23
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:21,760
and this economic recession
hit me hard.  It

24
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:25,840
occurred to me that this happens
to so many people when they go through a crisis,

25
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:28,160
that they are left with one hand in front
and the other behind,

26
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:30,840
and I had to do what you have
probably read

27
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:34,160
and heard many times,
reinvent your life.

28
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:36,560
Then, I had to reinvent it.

29
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:39,040
What I learned at that moment

30
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,160
was that I thought I
had my life figured out.

31
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:48,160
How many times do we think
that our life is already resolved?

32
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:52,080
Somehow, the fact of having
a more or less permanent job,

33
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:54,760
the fact of having a
more or less stable life,

34
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:58,040
the fact of having a life
with a certain comfort,

35
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:01,280
the fact that you do more or less the
same things every day,

36
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:04,000
the fact that you
are with the same people every day,

37
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:05,320
makes you believe

38
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:07,840
that your life is already resolved.

39
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:10,360
Happily resolved

40
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:13,920
and, instead,
your life changes in an instant.

41
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:16,480
So,
the first thing I learned was this.

42
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:20,320
Don't get too carried away
or lead your life

43
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:22,640
thinking that things are already done,

44
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:24,200
that you have them resolved,

45
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:26,800
because, as Mercedes Sosa sang,

46
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:29,360
life changes, everything changes.  And it

47
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:30,560
changes in an instant.

48
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:36,840
The second thing I learned,
oddly enough, was something unexpected.

49
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:41,680
I thought that, a little bit to foresee

50
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:43,400
what I could do in my life...

51
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:45,840
Because it's the first thing you ask yourself:
"And now, what?

52
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:47,160
What will I do?".

53
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:52,480
Well, it occurred to me,
instead of looking to the future, to

54
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:54,040
go back to the past.

55
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:57,720
I made an act of nostalgia,
but not painful nostalgia,

56
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:01,280
not nostalgia of this kind: "Oh,
how I miss those moments!",

57
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:05,440
but rather:
"What is it that I, as a child, as a young man, had

58
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:09,240
realized that I had a talent for,

59
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:11,280
that was useful for this,
that I really liked?".

60
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:14,200
So, I
often tell people

61
00:03:14,280 --> 00:03:16,560
when they come to the clinic
and are lost, I

62
00:03:16,640 --> 00:03:17,840
ask them this question:

63
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:20,440
"What did you like to do when you were young?

64
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:23,000
What were you good at?

65
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:25,240
What gave you happiness?"

66
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:27,160
Then,
I delved into this past

67
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:30,840
and found that, indeed,
around the age of 18, 17, 18,

68
00:03:30,920 --> 00:03:33,480
I already had a huge inclination
towards psychology.

69
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:38,280
He was that person to whom friends go
and tell them about the things that are happening to them.

70
00:03:38,360 --> 00:03:41,800
And I remember that,
at that moment, he showed

71
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:43,360
an enormous ability

72
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:47,280
to see the mechanisms
that were working in that person

73
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:50,040
so that they realized what
was happening to them.

74
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:51,600
So I learned another lesson.

75
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:54,800
No matter how much you tell
someone what to do, they

76
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:55,680
won't do it.

77
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:58,280
First, because you say so.

78
00:03:58,360 --> 00:04:00,520
And second, because you also don't understand

79
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:03,720
the mechanisms that move
this person to do what they do.

80
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:05,200
But it doesn't matter, it didn't matter.  They

81
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:07,920
must suffer whatever they must suffer.

82
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:10,680
They have to go their own way.

83
00:04:10,760 --> 00:04:13,320
And we
cannot stand in their way.

84
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:14,520
Then, sometimes, you get lost

85
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:17,240
because you want to follow
the same thing your peers do,

86
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:18,720
the same thing everyone else does.

87
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:20,640
Sometimes you go home to your family,

88
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:22,720
who tells you:
"No, stop being silly.

89
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:25,280
What's this show business? You'll
never make a living.

90
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:28,520
You have to be a civil servant,
that's what you have to be."

91
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:30,040
In my youth,

92
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:32,960
the two things
that all young people had to do

93
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:36,080
was work in an institution,

94
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:39,720
a bank or savings bank,

95
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:41,560
or as a civil servant.

96
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:43,600
This was our destiny.

97
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:47,200
And soon buy an apartment,
have a partner, get married,

98
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:49,240
have an apartment, start having children...

99
00:04:49,320 --> 00:04:52,440
This was the destiny that awaited us.

100
00:04:52,920 --> 00:04:55,200
And, instead...
I see you make faces like this...

101
00:04:56,040 --> 00:04:58,240
I think you older people understand me,

102
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:00,040
that you've had to go through
a bit of this.

103
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:02,080
Well, then...

104
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:04,560
No, no.

105
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:08,400
We must return to life

106
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:10,960
what life has given us as a gift.

107
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:15,120
As if it were something for us,
for us to develop

108
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:18,840
and then deliver it to life
in the best way we know how.

109
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:22,800
And lastly, don't set limitations on yourself,

110
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:24,840
especially not because of age.

111
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:28,600
Age is not in the body.

112
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:32,200
Yes, the body has its reasons, of course,

113
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:36,320
but we live according to this and according to that.

114
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:38,720
Therefore, there is no age.

115
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:40,320
Don't limit yourself, please, do

116
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:42,400
n't think
that your life is over,

117
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:45,360
don't think that, well,
what are you going to do with your life,

118
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:47,320
don't think that you're already grown up.

119
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:48,680
Please

120
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:53,800
give yourself what you think
life is asking of you, calling you,

121
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:55,840
and try it
even if you are at an age

122
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:57,720
where you feel like you are very old.

123
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:00,960
I repeat, this
is only on the ID card,

124
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:03,120
not in your life.

125
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:06,440
Your life,
I repeat, is here, above all, and here.

126
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:07,840
I am at your disposal.

127
00:06:19,160 --> 00:06:22,200
Hello, Xavier, delighted.
I am Reyes.

128
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:25,720
You said that one of your books
isThe Problem with Being Too Good.

129
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:29,160
I have two teenage children
who I think are.

130
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:32,360
When does being good
become a problem?

131
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:37,320
Well, Reyes, notice that in the consultation

132
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:40,760
I started exploring this topic

133
00:06:40,840 --> 00:06:44,200
when I realized
that different people were

134
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:45,640
telling me:

135
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:47,680
"You know what's wrong, Xavi?

136
00:06:47,760 --> 00:06:50,000
That my problem
is that I'm too good."

137
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:54,480
And this person told me that,
then another person told me that.

138
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:57,600
"My colleagues say
I'm too good a person.

139
00:06:57,680 --> 00:07:00,160
At work they tell me I'm too good."  Have they

140
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:01,720
told you?

141
00:07:02,240 --> 00:07:03,640
I see that yes,

142
00:07:03,720 --> 00:07:04,960
you say yes.

143
00:07:05,520 --> 00:07:08,680
Then, I started exploring.
What does it mean to be too good?

144
00:07:09,240 --> 00:07:10,680
This is where experience

145
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:14,800
led me to observe
a series of behavioral patterns

146
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:17,680
that are common
to all these people.

147
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:19,480
The first

148
00:07:20,440 --> 00:07:22,440
is a sense of duty.

149
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:27,440
I don't know if you were raised that way,
or if you had parents, father or mother,

150
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:29,400
who were very strict.

151
00:07:30,400 --> 00:07:31,920
What have they instilled in us?

152
00:07:32,600 --> 00:07:34,400
The sense of duty.

153
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:38,480
And what is it that one has
that is in one's duty?

154
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:40,280
Obey.

155
00:07:40,920 --> 00:07:46,000
Therefore, the fundamental principle
of this sense of duty is to obey.  I

156
00:07:47,120 --> 00:07:51,120
remember that at the consultation, not long ago,
when I started writing the book,

157
00:07:51,840 --> 00:07:54,640
a young girl left me frozen

158
00:07:55,320 --> 00:07:59,680
when she told me:
"I don't do anything unless I have to."

159
00:08:00,440 --> 00:08:01,240
"How?"

160
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:04,720
He says, "I function out of a sense of duty. I

161
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:07,480
just do what I have to do."

162
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:11,520
"But then, don't you do what you want?"

163
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:12,400
"Never."

164
00:08:13,480 --> 00:08:14,720
"But don't you have any desires?"

165
00:08:15,120 --> 00:08:15,920
"Few.

166
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,120
I've been taught since I was little
to do my duty. And I've gotten

167
00:08:20,520 --> 00:08:23,800
so used to it
that I do everything out of duty."

168
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:28,480
Wow, I was
very surprised because, of course, it

169
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:32,840
's shocking to see how
far

170
00:08:32,920 --> 00:08:36,440
this first commandment that we

171
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:38,360
good people receive,

172
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:41,760
"do your duty," can go.

173
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:43,280
Comply.

174
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:46,480
And what is your first compliment?
Obey.  And who should you

175
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,280
obey?
Your parents first.

176
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:52,000
Then, obey teachers,
obey people,

177
00:08:52,640 --> 00:08:55,480
obey those in charge, obey authority.

178
00:08:55,560 --> 00:08:58,080
Therefore, we have grown up with an order to

179
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:01,840
do everything well
because it is our duty.

180
00:09:02,280 --> 00:09:05,560
Therefore, the first commandment,
the first order,

181
00:09:05,640 --> 00:09:08,560
of what we would call being too good

182
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:10,680
is being too obedient.

183
00:09:11,640 --> 00:09:13,520
Because you know that being too obedient

184
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:16,080
means that, then, I do
n't know how to say no.

185
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:19,200
That I do everything that is expected,

186
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:23,200
that I respond
to everything that people want from me,

187
00:09:23,880 --> 00:09:27,560
that I have no limits,
much less for the rest.

188
00:09:27,640 --> 00:09:30,560
Everything is for the rest,
everything is what they ask of me.

189
00:09:31,840 --> 00:09:35,000
Therefore,
this first commandment is already very hard.

190
00:09:35,640 --> 00:09:37,840
But, be careful, there are also exceptions.  It

191
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:41,400
can also happen that, at home,
and I have also seen this,

192
00:09:42,160 --> 00:09:44,840
cases of people whose
homes were a mess.

193
00:09:45,880 --> 00:09:49,080
Disorder reigned, anarchy reigned,

195
00:09:51,680 --> 00:09:54,040
my parents didn't pay attention to me or anything,

196
00:09:54,120 --> 00:09:56,800
they did what they wanted,
they came in, they went out, they didn't give orders.

197
00:09:56,880 --> 00:10:00,480
So,
some boys and girls have suffered this.  They've

198
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:03,080
had such a bad time,
they've seen so much chaos

199
00:10:03,560 --> 00:10:06,880
that they've started
organizing themselves from a young age

200
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:10,160
to be good,
to do everything in an orderly manner.

201
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:15,400
Then, they grow up in an orderly manner,
even obsessively orderly,

202
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:20,600
so as not to relive
what made them suffer so much at home,

203
00:10:20,680 --> 00:10:23,840
which was that disorder,
that chaos in which they could not live.

204
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:24,840
First condition:

205
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:28,520
being excessively obedient.

206
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:31,520
The second condition, Reyes,

207
00:10:32,200 --> 00:10:35,840
consists of the command "behave well."

208
00:10:36,440 --> 00:10:38,440
Haven't they told you to "behave"?

209
00:10:39,080 --> 00:10:42,640
And when you're older,
when you're with your parents,

210
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:44,960
when you leave home,
even though you're 50 years old,

211
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:46,960
don't they tell you to "be good"?

212
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:50,840
So, this command, "behave well,"

213
00:10:51,400 --> 00:10:53,720
is a command that indicates, precisely,

214
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:56,960
not only that you must obey,
but how you must obey.

215
00:10:57,360 --> 00:11:00,640
Then, "behave yourself"
includes life scripts.

216
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:02,720
And life scripts include:

217
00:11:04,520 --> 00:11:05,640
"Be accommodating,

218
00:11:06,600 --> 00:11:08,440
do everything right,

219
00:11:09,520 --> 00:11:11,240
do everything perfect,

220
00:11:12,080 --> 00:11:12,880
don't cry,

221
00:11:14,600 --> 00:11:15,440
go fast,

222
00:11:16,120 --> 00:11:18,280
please, don't say no."

223
00:11:19,320 --> 00:11:22,680
Therefore, there is
a whole series of orders that the child receives

224
00:11:22,760 --> 00:11:24,880
and that he grows up with,

225
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:27,880
dragging precisely
these life scripts.  They

226
00:11:27,960 --> 00:11:29,360
are complacent.  They do

227
00:11:30,120 --> 00:11:33,400
everything so that their child,
their children,

228
00:11:33,480 --> 00:11:36,320
their partner,
their family are well.

229
00:11:37,640 --> 00:11:38,840
But notice that a person

230
00:11:38,920 --> 00:11:41,280
who spends his life
pleasing others

231
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:45,080
comes to a point where he
neglects his own desires.  I

232
00:11:45,160 --> 00:11:47,560
remember a gentleman
who came to the presentation

233
00:11:48,160 --> 00:11:51,680
and said: "I like to be accommodating.

234
00:11:52,880 --> 00:11:54,520
Please don't take that away from me."

235
00:11:55,920 --> 00:11:59,000
"Okay,
if you are happy to please, that's fine.

236
00:11:59,080 --> 00:12:02,360
But do you also ask to be
pleased?"

237
00:12:02,840 --> 00:12:03,920
"Oh, no, not that."

238
00:12:05,040 --> 00:12:06,480
Then, we have a problem.

239
00:12:07,160 --> 00:12:09,320
You give, you give,

240
00:12:09,880 --> 00:12:11,320
but you don't receive,

241
00:12:11,400 --> 00:12:13,840
and that's already very dangerous.

242
00:12:14,560 --> 00:12:15,640
What does it mean?

243
00:12:15,720 --> 00:12:18,480
From so much pleasing, there comes a time when

244
00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:21,400
I become
the eyes of others,

245
00:12:21,480 --> 00:12:22,880
the eyes of my family.

246
00:12:24,280 --> 00:12:26,520
I think some people will
feel reflected in it.

247
00:12:28,960 --> 00:12:30,080
You get home:

248
00:12:30,160 --> 00:12:33,160
"Ugh, this hair, I'll call
the hairdresser to fix it

249
00:12:33,240 --> 00:12:34,320
because you don't wear it very well.

250
00:12:35,160 --> 00:12:38,040
Ugh, son, these shoes... We
need to buy other shoes. We'll

251
00:12:38,120 --> 00:12:40,120
buy new shoes tomorrow."

252
00:12:40,680 --> 00:12:42,080
It's your eyes.  They

253
00:12:43,160 --> 00:12:44,320
see everything.  They

254
00:12:45,280 --> 00:12:47,200
always see everything that is missing.

255
00:12:47,720 --> 00:12:49,800
They see everything that is needed.

256
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:51,760
They see everything that is necessary

257
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:54,720
and buy it for you and make it for you.

258
00:12:56,560 --> 00:12:59,160
And there comes a time
when you have to tell them,

259
00:12:59,240 --> 00:13:03,280
"Please, thank you, but I want to do
things my way."

260
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:06,600
"But, for God's sake,

261
00:13:07,480 --> 00:13:11,040
how can you say that to me?
If I do everything for you."

262
00:13:12,280 --> 00:13:13,080
"Yeah,

263
00:13:13,920 --> 00:13:16,800
you do everything for me, but according to you,

264
00:13:17,480 --> 00:13:18,440
not according to me."

265
00:13:19,680 --> 00:13:23,080
"But if no one
knows what suits you better than me

266
00:13:23,160 --> 00:13:24,760
and knows what's best for you."

267
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:26,520
Because, in the end, they end up telling you this.

268
00:13:27,560 --> 00:13:31,600
Then, notice how this person has
become disconnected from themselves

269
00:13:31,680 --> 00:13:33,560
and ends up living the life of the other person.

270
00:13:34,280 --> 00:13:37,080
And she ends up living and making the other person live

271
00:13:37,160 --> 00:13:39,800
according to what she considers to be
best for the other person.

272
00:13:39,880 --> 00:13:41,560
And you can't blame him

273
00:13:41,640 --> 00:13:43,600
because, if you do, then he gets angry

274
00:13:43,680 --> 00:13:45,440
because he's doing everything for you.  Do

275
00:13:46,400 --> 00:13:48,560
you realize the extremes
that can be reached

276
00:13:48,640 --> 00:13:50,640
when a person is not only complacent,

277
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:54,040
but becomes necessary
to the rest of the world?

278
00:13:55,840 --> 00:13:58,160
Therefore, this command to "behave"

279
00:13:58,840 --> 00:14:00,000
can go a long way.

280
00:14:01,960 --> 00:14:05,440
Third commandment:
the impossibility of stopping being good.  You do

281
00:14:06,760 --> 00:14:09,960
n't know how many times
I've told someone in the office:

282
00:14:10,040 --> 00:14:10,920
"But please

283
00:14:11,640 --> 00:14:14,560
